{
  // ==========================================================================
  //  tsconfig.typecheck.json — THE TYPE-CHECK OF THE SHIPPED DECLARATION
  //  (`DEF-2026-10-16-Q`: `bindings/js/index.d.ts` SHIPS TO CONSUMERS and
  //  nothing in this repository checked it; this file and the fixture beside
  //  it are that instrument, driven by `npm run typecheck`. JSONC: `tsc`
  //  reads comments in a tsconfig, and the reasoning belongs beside the
  //  settings it justifies.)
  //
  //  WHAT IT CHECKS, AND THE ONE THING IT MUST NOT DO
  //  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  The subject is `bindings/js/index.d.ts` — the file `bindings/js/package.json`'s
  //  `types` field reaches and its `files` allowlist ships. The fixture
  //  (`typecheck/consumer-fixture.ts`) reaches it THE WAY A CONSUMER DOES:
  //
  //      import { storeCreate } from 'provident-storeroom';
  //
  //  i.e. by the PACKAGE SPECIFIER, never by a relative path into the tree. A
  //  relative import would bypass the manifest entirely and would keep passing
  //  after the manifest itself broke.
  //
  //  ⟶ AND THE RESOLUTION MODE IS A MEASURED CHOICE, NOT A DEFAULT — `node10`.
  //  Measured on this machine (`typescript@5.9.3`, the pinned devDependency):
  //  under `moduleResolution: "nodenext"` resolution walks the `exports` map,
  //  matches `import` → `./index.mjs`, looks for `index.d.mts` beside it, finds
  //  none, and REFUSES; `bundler` fails identically. `bindings/js/package.json`
  //  carries no `types` condition INSIDE its `exports` map (only the top-level
  //  `types` field), so THE SHIPPED DECLARATION IS UNREACHABLE TO A `nodenext`
  //  CONSUMER. That is a real finding about the manifest, recorded here and NOT
  //  fixed by this instrument (`bindings/js/package.json`'s `exports` map is
  //  outside this task's write wall). `node10` is the mode that reads the
  //  `types` field — the routing the package actually declares — so it is the
  //  mode that checks the shipped bytes today.
  //
  //  WHAT A GREEN RUN PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
  //  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  GREEN PROVES: the declaration is INTERNALLY CONSISTENT (it compiles); a
  //  consumer's calls against the surface it declares — the ONE export, the
  //  TWELVE own members, each method's arity and argument order, `write`'s
  //  optional fourth-position commit, the declared return shapes — would
  //  COMPILE; and the calls the declaration must REFUSE are refused (the
  //  `@ts-expect-error` cells).
  //  GREEN DOES **NOT** PROVE THE DECLARATION MATCHES THE RUNTIME. A `.d.ts`
  //  that said `read` takes a `number` where the implementation takes an array
  //  would type-check green all day: `tsc` never loads `index.mjs`, never loads
  //  the addon, and never compares a declaration to a value. The runtime's own
  //  agreement is the node suite's subject (`npm test`), not this instrument's.
  //  Neither does it prove the THREE CLAIMS the declaration's own header names
  //  as unenforceable (supplied-vs-omitted `undefined` at the tier position;
  //  non-negative-integer address segments; the twelve-own-member COUNT) — the
  //  fixture does not assert those, and says so in its own bytes.
  //
  //  WHY THE FIXTURE LIVES UNDER `typecheck/` AND NOT UNDER `bindings/js/`
  //  -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //  `bindings/js/package.json`'s `files` allowlist is exactly
  //  `[index.mjs, README.md, index.d.ts, src/constraint-kernel.mjs]` and must
  //  not move: anything placed under `bindings/js/` risks being swept into the
  //  published tarball, and this instrument must never ship. `typecheck/` is at
  //  the ROOT, beside this file, and the root manifest is `private: true`.
  // ==========================================================================
  "compilerOptions": {
    // THE DECLARATION IS A CONSUMER-FACING CONTRACT: file it at strict mode,
    // because that is what a consumer's own project is likely to be.
    "strict": true,
    // NO EMIT: this is a check, never a build. A fixture that wrote a `.js`
    // beside itself would be an artefact of an instrument, not a deliverable.
    "noEmit": true,
    // The mode that reads `bindings/js/package.json`'s `types` field — see the
    // measured note above for why `nodenext`/`bundler` cannot reach the
    // declaration at all.
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "node10",
    "target": "es2022",
    "lib": ["es2022"],
    // The fixture is a CONSUMER project: it must not lean on ambient typing the
    // package itself would never ship (`@types/node` and friends).
    "types": [],
    // And it must not silently accept a module that resolved to `any`: an
    // unsilenced `any` is exactly the failure mode this instrument exists to
    // catch (`TS7016`, "no declaration file for module").
    "skipLibCheck": false,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true
  },
  // The fixture only. There is no `src/**/*.ts` in this repository to sweep in:
  // the implementation is Rust plus authored JavaScript (`.mjs`), and `checkJs`
  // is deliberately NOT enabled — this instrument's subject is the DECLARATION.
  "include": ["typecheck/**/*.ts"]
}
